<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henri Matisse</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1869">1869</a>–<a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Matisse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henri Matisse was a French painter, one of the leading figures of modern art in the twentieth century. Along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso">Picasso</a> and others, he admired African art that reached European collections. The site cites this admiration in discussing how the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a> influenced European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modernism">modernism</a>, part of the legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henri-matisse.jpg" alt="Henri Matisse" width="320" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alvin Langdon Coburn · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henri_Matisse,_1913,_photograph_by_Alvin_Langdon_Coburn.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henri Émile Benoît Matisse was born on 31 December 1869 in Le Cateau-Cambrésis in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the oldest son of a wealthy grain merchant. In 1887 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to study law and afterwards worked as a court administrator. He began to paint in 1889, when his mother brought him art supplies while he was convalescing from appendicitis, and decided to become an artist, which deeply disappointed his father. In 1891 he returned to Paris to study at the Académie Julian under William-Adolphe Bouguereau and at the École Nationale des Beaux-Arts under Gustave Moreau.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The intensity of colour in the works Matisse painted between 1900 and 1905 brought him notoriety as one of the Fauves, French for wild beasts. The movement lasted only from 1904 to 1908, and he and André Derain were its leaders. In 1904 he painted at St. Tropez with the neo-Impressionists Signac and Henri-Edmond Cross, and in 1905 he worked with Derain at Collioure. Around April 1906 he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso">Pablo Picasso</a>, eleven years younger, and the two became lifelong friends and rivals. Americans in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, among them the Stein family and the Cone sisters of Baltimore, collected his work.</p><p>In 1917 Matisse moved to Cimiez, a suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>. His work of the next decade relaxed and softened, though some critics found his odalisque paintings shallow and decorative, and after 1930 a bolder simplification appeared. He was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> when the Germans invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in June 1940 but returned to Nice and stayed, though his son Pierre begged him to leave.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Diagnosed with abdominal cancer in 1941, Matisse underwent surgery that left him often bedridden. With assistants he began to make cut paper collages from sheets pre-painted with gouache, and the pieces grew into murals and room-sized works. In 1948 he began designs for the Chapelle du Rosaire at Vence, and after 1951 the paper cut-out was his only medium. He called his last years his second life. He died of a heart attack on 3 November 1954, aged 84, and is buried at Cimiez. The Matisse Museum at Le Cateau was established in 1952 and the Musée Matisse in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> opened in 1963.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henri Matisse” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fauvism and Cubism arose in the studios of <b>Henri Matisse</b>, Pablo Picasso and Georges Braque between 1900 and 1910; in 1907 Picasso painted Les Demoiselles d’Avignon and Oskar Kokoschka wrote the first Expressionist play, and Arnold Schoenberg’s Second String Quartet of 1908 was his first composition without a…</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Le Cateau-Cambrésis, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.11,3.54&amp;year=1924" aria-label="Le Cateau-Cambrésis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Henri Matisse"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5194 5837h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5194 5837h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.11,3.54&amp;year=1924">What was here in 1924? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
